


The Virtual Machine "ari.h"

            The ari.h header declares the operations of the
            virtual machine and other functions that support
            those operations.

    Actions on All Cores

        mari_reset

            The mari_reset function resets all cores. When
            called for the first time, memory is allocated
            for the cores.

        mari_unset

            The mari_unset function is intended to be called
            during program termination. The memory
            initially allocated by mari_reset is freed.

        mari_report_awake

            The mari_report_awake function writes to the
            standard error stream a representation of the
            awake state of each core.

        mari_rotate

            The mari_rotate function activates the next
            core. The mari_rotate function is intended to be
            called when the current core is not awake.

            If no cores are awake, the mari_rotate function
            awakens and activates the primary core.

    Actions on Individual Cores

        ari_cold

            The ari_cold function restarts the current node
            as if it were starting up just after launch or
            power-up.

        ari_warm

            The ari_warm function restarts the current node
            while keeping certain elements intact, such as
            any definitions.

                                 * * * *

        ari_is_awake

            The ari_is_awake function tells whether the
            active virtual machine is in an awake state.

        ari_sleep

            The ari_sleep function clears the awake flag of
            the current node, switches to the primary node,
            and sets the awake flag of the primary node.

        ari_pause

            The ari_pause function switches to the primary
            node.

        ari_focus

            The ari_focus function makes the node specified
            on the stack the current node.

                                 * * * *

        ari_wstack_dump

            The ari_wstack_dump function writes to the
            standard error stream the words on the word
            stack.

        ari_query

            The ari_query function rotates the word stack
            and replaces the new top word with the next word
            from the standard input stream.

                                 * * * *

        ari_interpret

            The ari_interpret function interprets the word
            on the top of the word stack as a mnemonic.

                                 * * * *

        ari_opcode_from_mnemonic

            The ari_opcode_from_mnemonic function pops a
            word from the word stack and pushes the
            corresponding op-code onto the stack, then
            pushes a flag onto the stack. The flag tells
            whether the word matched a mnemonic (zero
            indicates a match or success).

                                 * * * *

        ari_mnemonics_dump

            The ari_mnemonics_dump function writes to the
            standard error stream the mnemonics
            corresponding to the op-codes and basic operations.

                                 * * * *

        ari_nop

            The ari_nop function does nothing except waste
            time.

    The Data Stack

            There are two special registers which often
            behave as if they were the top two elements of
            the data stack. These two registers interact
            with a true stack. In this section, the
            registers are called T (top) and S (second).

            T and S store either a numeric value from 0 to
            UCHAR_MAX. T and S are nullable so that T and S,
            together with the true stack together may be
            viewed as a single stack.

            If T stores a numeric value 0 to UCHAR_MAX, we
            say that T is populated. Otherwise, we say that
            T is unpopulated. Similarly for S.

        ari_stack_dump

            The ari_stack_dump function writes to the
            standard error stream a representation of the
            stack, including the stack depth, the number of
            empty slots, the values of the registers, and
            the values on the stack proper.

        ari_dup

            ( x -- x x )

            The ari_dup function behaves as follows:---

            If T and S are populated, S is pushed onto the
            true stack. Then, T is copied into S. Thus, T is
            duplicated.

            Otherwise, if T is populated, but S is not, T is
            copied unto S.

            Otherwise, neither T nor S is populated; T and S
            are each set to zero.

        ari_drop

            ( x -- )

            The ari_drop function copies S into T. Then, if
            the true stack is populated, the true stack is
            popped and the popped value is copied onto S.

        ari_over

            ( a b -- a b a )
            (     or       )
            (   b -- 0 b 0 )
            (     or       )
            (     -- 0 0 0 )

            The ari_over function behaves a follows:---

            If both T and S are populated, The value of S is
            stored---call the stored value R. Then, S is
            pushed; T is copied into S; and R is copied into
            T.

            Otherwise, if T is populated but not S; the
            ari_over function behaves as if the value of S
            were zero.

            Otherwise, neither T nor S is populated; the
            ari_over function behaves as if both values were
            zero.

        ari_swap

            ( a b -- b a )
            (     or     )
            (   b -- b 0 )
            (     or     )
            (     -- 0 0 )

            The ari_swap function behaves as follows:---

            If both T ans S are populated, their values are
            swapped.

            Otherwise, if T is populated but S is not, the
            ari_swap function behaves as if the value of S
            were zero.

            Otherise, neither T nor S is populated, and no
            action is taken. Conceptually, this may also be
            viewed as swapping two null values.

                                 * * * *

        ari_zero

            ( x y -- 0 0 )
            (     or     )
            (   x -- 0 0 )
            (     or     )
            (     -- 0 0 )

            The ari_zero function sets both S and T to zero.
            This is a destructive operation.

        ari_add

            ( a b -- s c )

            The ari_add behaves as follows:---

            The ari_add function affects only T and S.

            If either T or S are empty, the ari_add function
            behaves as if they were first set to zero.

            The sum of T and S is computed and T and S are
            overwritten with the sum: the least significant
            byte in S and the most significant bit (the
            carry bit) in T.

        ari_nand

            ( a b -- c c )

            The ari_nand behaves as follows:---

            The ari_nand function affects only T and S.

            If either T or S are empty, the ari_add function
            behaves as if they were first set to zero.

            T NAND S is computed and the result is stored in
            both S and T. Thus, ari_nand does not affect the
            stack, proper.
